{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar link-audit --repair` unconditionally aborts with \"a reconciler pass is in flight (refs/reconciler/lock held or unreadable) — refusing to repair\", even when no reconciler pass is running. The guard never reads the lock at all.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`link_audit._reconciler_in_flight` (`src/rebar/_commands/link_audit.py:216`) delegates to `fsck_repair._reconciler_in_flight` (`src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py:227`), which probes via:\n\n```\nfrom rebar_reconciler import _advisory_lock as advisory\n```\n\n`rebar_reconciler` is NOT importable from an in-process CLI invocation. It lives at `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler`, and `src/rebar/_engine` is deliberately not a package — the directory is put on `PYTHONPATH` by `engine_env` (`src/rebar/_engine.py:75`), whose own docstring states it is \"the ONLY place the engine dir is put on an import path — and it is scoped to subprocesses, never the in-process library path\". The CLI applies it only when shelling out for the `reconcile` arm (`src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py:148-160`).\n\nSo the import raises `ModuleNotFoundError`, the function takes its fail-CLOSED branch, and returns True regardless of actual lock state. Verified directly: `_reconciler_in_flight(\".\")` returns True while `from rebar_reconciler import _advisory_lock` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`.\n\nThe fail-closed POLICY is correct — an indeterminate lock must not permit a destructive repair. The defect is that the probe cannot reach a determinate answer from the context it is called in, so \"indeterminate\" is the only outcome it can produce.\n\n## Impact\n\n- `link-audit --repair` (shipped in 9ad7-d5a2-a664-4827) is unusable, so the 18 legacy bad edges in this repo's tracker cannot be repaired and story affe-2b42-4ee4-4e12 cannot meet its success criteria.\n- `fsck --repair` calls the SAME function and is affected identically. That path predates this work, so this is a latent pre-existing bug that `link-audit` surfaced rather than introduced.\n- No test exercises `_reconciler_in_flight`, which is why it shipped broken; `grep -rn _reconciler_in_flight tests/` returns nothing.\n\n## Approach\n\nProbe the lock in a SUBPROCESS launched with `engine_env`, rather than importing the engine in-process.\n\nThis is the only option that respects the architecture. The whole lock stack is engine-internal: `check_pass_lock` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_advisory_lock.py:116`) delegates to `_ref_lock.read(...)`, itself lazy-loaded by file path from inside the engine, and the read consults the configured sync remote. Putting that on the in-process import path is precisely what `engine_env`'s contract forbids, and it is also how the CLI already reaches the reconciler.\n\nFix it in `fsck_repair._reconciler_in_flight`, the single shared implementation, so `fsck --repair` and `link-audit --repair` are both corrected by one change. `link_audit._reconciler_in_flight` keeps delegating and needs no edit.\n\nThe subprocess runs `sys.executable -c` with `env=engine_env(root)` and maps its exit status onto a tri-state:\n\n- exit 0 → lock free → return False (repair may proceed);\n- exit 2 → `check_pass_lock` returned True → lock held → return True;\n- any other exit, a raise inside the child, `OSError`, or a timeout → indeterminate → return True (fail-CLOSED, unchanged policy).\n\nA bounded timeout is applied so a hung probe cannot wedge the repair; the timeout is itself treated as indeterminate.\n\nRejected alternative: re-implement the lease check in `rebar._store` by parsing `refs/reconciler/lock` directly. The blob is simple (`{fence, heartbeat_ns, holder, lease_secs}`), but the authoritative read also resolves the configured sync remote and owns corrupt-blob and timeout semantics. Duplicating it would fork the lease rule between engine and CLI, and the two would drift silently — exactly the failure mode `_relations._BLOCKING_RELATIONS` exists to prevent elsewhere in this codebase.\n\nRejected alternative: add the engine dir to `sys.path` in-process. Smallest diff, but it breaches the documented boundary at `src/rebar/_engine.py:78-80` and would make the engine importable from every command, which that comment exists to prevent.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` — replace the in-process import inside `_reconciler_in_flight` (`:227`) with the `engine_env` subprocess probe and its tri-state exit mapping. The fail-closed default and the function's signature/return contract are unchanged, so both call sites (`:309` and `link_audit`) are untouched.\n- `tests/unit/test_reconciler_in_flight.py` — new test module; the function currently has no coverage at all.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: with no lock ref present, the probe returns False and a repair is permitted — this is the case that is impossible today.\n- Edge: with the lock held, the probe returns True.\n- Failure: a child that exits non-zero for any other reason returns True (fail-closed).\n- Failure: a child that raises inside `check_pass_lock` returns True.\n- Failure: a subprocess timeout returns True rather than propagating.\n- Failure: `OSError` launching the interpreter returns True.\n- Regression: the probe never imports `rebar_reconciler` in-process — asserted by confirming the import still fails in-process while the probe returns a determinate answer, which is the exact contradiction that proves the bug fixed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_reconciler_in_flight` returns False on a store with no reconciler lock ref; verified by the new happy-path case in `tests/unit/test_reconciler_in_flight.py`, which fails against the pre-fix implementation.\n- [ ] `_reconciler_in_flight` returns True when `check_pass_lock` reports the lock held; verified by the held-lock case.\n- [ ] Each indeterminate path — non-zero child exit, a raise inside the child, a timeout, and an `OSError` on launch — returns True; verified by four failure cases asserting fail-closed is preserved.\n- [ ] `from rebar_reconciler import _advisory_lock` still raises `ModuleNotFoundError` in-process while the probe returns a determinate answer; verified by the regression case, proving the fix did not work by loosening the boundary.\n- [ ] `rebar link-audit --repair` proceeds past the guard against this repository's tracker when no reconciler pass is running; verified by running it and observing the `pre-link-audit-repair` tag created, rather than the in-flight abort.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_reconciler_in_flight.py tests/scripts/graph/`, `make lint` and `make typecheck` all pass; verified by running each."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785386327775769001,"uuid":"0398c4b3-7e4a-4806-a071-643423da299b"}